[For "white"]
[Kind "main"]
[Start "sicilian-main 2.c3"]
[Name "2...Nf6: chase the knight"]

2... Nf6 { Hits e4, and tries to take advantage of the fact that 2.c3 took the knight's best square. [%cal Rf6e4] }
3. e5 { Pushes the knight away with a gain of time. There is no pawn on d6 yet, so e5 is safe. [%cal Ge5f6] }
3... Nd5
4. d4 { The plan as usual: take the centre now the knight has moved. }
4... cxd4
5. cxd4 { The c-pawn takes back, and White has pawns on d4 and e5. }
5... Nc6
6. Nf3 { Develops and guards both centre pawns. }
6... d6 { The right way to hit the centre. }
( 6... Nb6? { Goes back before hitting the centre, and now nothing stops the d-pawn. }
  7. d5 { Hits the c6 knight, and it cannot take e5, because Nxe5 would win it. [%cal Gd5c6] }
  7... Nb4 8. Nc3 e6 9. d6 { Black's pieces are tangled and the pawn on d6 cuts the board in two: the engine has White about five pawns ahead. [%csl Gd6] } )
7. Bc4 { Hits the d5 knight. The pawn on d6 has cut the queen off from it, so it has to move again. [%cal Gc4d5] [%csl Yd5] }
7... Nb6
8. Bb5 { Pins the c6 knight, the piece that guards e5 and hits d4. [%cal Gb5e8] [%icon pin@c6] [%idea pin] }
8... dxe5
9. Nxe5 { Takes back. The pinned knight on c6 cannot take. }
9... Bd7 { Gets ready to break the pin. }
( 9... Qc7? { Hits the knight on e5, but it leaves the queen on the same line as White's bishop. [%cal Gc1f4] }
  10. Bf4 { Guards the knight and lines up on the queen, so the pin on c6 keeps biting. No material is won yet, but the engine has White nearly three pawns ahead. [%cal Gf4c7] } )
( 9... e6? { A natural move, but it leaves the pinned knight short of a defender. }
  10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. Bxc6+ { The pawn that took back has opened the way: check, and the bishop hits the rook on a8 as well. [%cal Gc6a8] [%idea fork] }
  11... Bd7 12. Bxa8 { After ...Qxa8 White has won a rook and a pawn for a bishop. } )
10. Nxd7 { Removes the piece that would unpin. }
10... Qxd7
( 10... Nxd7? { Takes with the wrong piece: the queen stays at home and the c6 knight stays pinned. [%icon pin@c6] }
  11. d5 { The pawn hits the pinned knight. }
  11... Nce5 12. Bf4 { Black's knights are tied up in the middle of the board. No material is won yet, but the engine has White nearly two pawns ahead. } )
11. Nc3 { Brings out the last piece. }
11... e6
12. O-O { The game is level, but White's pieces are easy to play: the plan is Be3, then Rc1 and pressure on the c-file. The d-pawn is isolated, so it wants piece support. [%idea isolated-queens-pawn] } *
